WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.940
Start cloud inference with one model and one measured request path.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.680
Download model artifacts from an approved source, verify the expected version,

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.720
and place them in storage appropriate to their reuse.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.155
Quantization can reduce VRAM demand and make a less expensive instance viable,

00:00:14.155 --> 00:00:15.640
but it may affect quality.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.430
Benchmark candidate quantizations with prompts and context lengths that represent the

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:20.920
intended application.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.915
A serving API separates clients from the model process.

00:00:23.915 --> 00:00:26.810
Configure authentication, request limits, timeouts, health checks,

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.720
and structured errors before treating it as a shared service.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:33.880
Concurrency increases the number of simultaneous sequences and their memory demand.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:37.825
Batching may improve accelerator utilization and total throughput but can increase the

00:00:37.825 --> 00:00:39.600
latency of an individual request.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.965
Measure time to first token, prompt processing, generation rate, queue time,

00:00:42.965 --> 00:00:45.085
and completed requests over time.

00:00:45.085 --> 00:00:46.100
Network distance matters.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.625
A fast model in a distant region may feel slower than a modest model placed near its users

00:00:50.625 --> 00:00:52.040
and application data.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.365
Include gateway, queue, and transfer delays in end-to-end tests.

00:00:55.365 --> 00:00:57.690
Exercise long prompts, multiple clients, failures,

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.905
and restart behavior rather than relying on a single short request.

00:01:00.905 --> 00:01:04.140
Autoscaling can add or remove instances according to demand,

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.575
but GPU startup may be slow because capacity must be allocated, software initialized,

00:01:08.575 --> 00:01:09.615
and models loaded.

00:01:09.615 --> 00:01:14.240
Define minimum capacity, scale triggers, cooldown periods, and maximum cost exposure.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.160
Autoscaling is not a substitute for capacity planning or admission control.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.465
Establish a stable single-instance baseline first,

00:01:20.465 --> 00:01:24.210
then scale when real workload measurements show where additional resources improve

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:24.710
service.
